# Suppressions for reachable memory that the glibc dynamic linker (ld.so)
# holds when a name lookup that needs DNS makes glibc load an NSS service
# module (libnss_dns.so.2, libnss_mdns4_minimal.so.2, ...). The chain is
# __nss_module_load -> module_load -> _dl_open, and you can reproduce it with
# nothing but a getaddrinfo(3) call on a name that the hosts file doesn't
# answer: such a program leaks the same seven reachable records as this
# suite's own tests binary.
#
# These blocks are ld.so's internal bookkeeping (link_map structures,
# strdup'd library path strings from the ld.so cache lookup, and
# symbol-version tables), and they stay live until process exit on purpose:
# glibc loads NSS modules through its own private loader
# (__libc_dlopen_mode) and never calls a matching dlclose-equivalent. They
# aren't leaks.
#
# Two rules cover every variant, split only by which allocator ld.so uses,
# and both are exercised. The malloc rule covers the cache-lookup path
# strings and _dl_new_object's own link_map allocation, and the calloc rule
# covers _dl_new_object's larger zeroed object block and
# _dl_check_map_versions's version tables.
#
# Each rule requires _dl_open itself in the stack, and that keeps them away
# from anything we allocate: these test binaries link the library
# statically, so none of our code ever runs underneath a dynamic-linker
# open.

{
   ld_so_dlopen_reachable_malloc
   Memcheck:Leak
   match-leak-kinds: reachable
   fun:malloc
   ...
   fun:_dl_open
}

{
   ld_so_dlopen_reachable_calloc
   Memcheck:Leak
   match-leak-kinds: reachable
   fun:calloc
   ...
   fun:_dl_open
}
